  "rationale": "The binding constraints are throughput >= 50 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-12; power is minimised subject to those, and the front is scored on power vs accuracy_bits. Accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, so all four families share the same accuracy box: output LSB is 2^-(W-2), meaning 2^-12 max error forces W around 14+ (13 is the boundary case) and N around 14-16, hence data_width 13..18 and n_iter 13..20 centred on the wall rather than on generous accuracy. Rounding is left open (trunc vs round) because round buys a bit of accuracy for free where it lets W drop by one. Throughput separates the families: pipelined and pipelined_m give 1 result/cycle and clear 50 MSPS trivially, unrolled_k needs k large enough that ceil(N/k)+3 cycles still hits 50 MSPS, and iterative needs an implausible ~800 MHz for the N required, so it is only a small probe. Budget is weighted to pipelined_m and unrolled_k because they are the plausible low-power feasible designs (fewer pipeline registers; time-multiplexed datapath), with pipelined retained to anchor the high-power end of the trade-off curve that hypervolume needs, and iterative kept minimal to either confirm infeasibility or catch an edge case. This first round deliberately spans the full m/k sweeps so the reserved later budget can map the front over the ranges of whichever family ends up carrying it."
